Chapter I – Familiar Face 

The console room was quiet. 

The rotor turned over slow and easy, and the roundels glowed their soft, patient glow, and the Doctor stood at the console with both hands flat on the edge of it, looking up at the ceiling. 

It went up a very long way. It always had. 

A smile found its way onto his face — not the enormous one, not the one that came out at the sight of a new sky. A smaller thing than that, and considerably heavier. 

"I don't want to do this alone," he said. 

He said it into the empty air, and the air, being the air of that particular ship, took it the way a good friend takes a confession: without fuss, and without pretending not to have heard. The lights along the gantry dipped. Just slightly. Just once. 

He pushed off from the console and walked, and his stride was light — lighter than it had any business being — the metal gangway ringing under his feet the same six notes it always rang, in the same order. 

The doors stood open at the end of it. 

Beyond them there was no ground, no city, no small furious person demanding to know what he thought he was playing at. There was only space, and a nebula sprawled across the whole of it in crimson and violet and great slow rivers of gold. 

He stopped in the doorway and set a hand against the frame. The wood was warm. It was always warm. 

"I'm never alone with you," he said, and the smile broadened, and for a moment there was nothing sad in it at all. 

Then the joy came up through him like weather. 

He shoved the doors wide with both hands and leaned out into the starlight, out over a drop that meant precisely nothing to him, and shouted it at the nebula and the stars and anyone at all who happened to be listening: 

"Joy to the world!" 

And he laughed. 

He stepped out onto the ledge of the police box — an ordinary painted step, made for milkmen and constables, presently sixteen thousand light-years from the nearest pavement — and the fire started in his hands. 

A prickle first, then a warmth, then that old familiar heat unspooling out of his wrists and pooling into his palms, curling round his fingers like something molten deciding to be affectionate about it. Golden. It was always so golden. 

He held his hands out in front of him and looked at them, blazing. 

"And that is exactly the word," he said. 

Then he looked back into the heart of his ship — one last time, and he knew it was the last time, and he let himself know it. His eyes were shining wet. His grin didn't waver by so much as a millimetre. 

"This has been," he said, "an absolute joy." 

Then he flung his arms wide to the cosmos, and let go. 

The light went out of him in a torrent, tearing out of his chest and his throat and his outstretched hands, shredding away into the dark. The fire took his silhouette and swallowed it whole, and inside the blaze his face began to come apart — softening, smoothing, the old lines dissolving into brightness. 

And then, as it always does, the fire went out. 

The last of it curled away in bright drifting motes, and standing where he had stood was a woman with blonde hair blazing in the nebula-light, blinking hard, both hands half-raised, wearing an expression of pure astonishment. 

He drew in a sharp breath. A grin broke over his face like sunrise over a hill. 

"Oh, hello!" he gasped. 

Then he was moving — back through the doors, back into the light of the console room, hands already up at his face. Jaw. Cheekbones. Mouth. The whole unfamiliar architecture of it, pressed and prodded and read like braille. Nose, present. Eyes, two of them, a good start. Hair — rather a lot of hair, and getting in the way. 

He stopped. 

Because it was familiar. 

Not familiar the way an old face is familiar, not one of his own worn a second time round with the corners rubbed off. Something else. Something he knew the shape of without being able to say from where. 

He stood very still in the middle of his ship, with both hands on a face that wasn't new at all, and waited for the name to arrive. 

The TARDIS gave him the mirror. 

It swung out of the wall on its little brass arm — the round shaving mirror that had followed her through six console rooms and had never once, in all that time, been asked for. She'd kept it. She'd been keeping it. 

He looked. 

And for a moment there was nothing, only a stranger looking back with his own startled expression on her face, and he thought: well, that's alright. New nose. Manageable hair. Could have been worse, could have had the ears again — 

And then it landed. 

It didn't arrive the way a name usually arrives, filed and fetched and handed over. It came the way a smell comes: all at once, sideways, dragging a whole decade in behind it. A council estate. A hand grabbing his in a basement full of shop dummies. Run. A beach in Norway with the wind taking every word that mattered. 

"Rose," he said. 

And it was her voice that said it. 

She heard it happen — heard the shape of the name in her own mouth, in that flat, warm, unmistakable London vowel — and her hand came up over it as though she could push the word back in and start again. 

"Rose Tyler," she said, to the mirror, to the ship, to nobody. "You've given me Rose Tyler." 

The rotor turned over slow and easy, and said nothing, and was not the least bit sorry. 

Because that was the question, and it arrived about four seconds after. 

Not what. She could live with what. She'd been young and old and tall and short and Scottish and, on one occasion she preferred not to revisit, briefly convinced she was a lorry driver from Rotherham. The what had never mattered very much. 

Why. 

Regeneration doesn't invent a face. That was the thing. Down in all that howling cellular chaos there is no workshop, no committee, nobody sketching cheekbones — it doesn't build, it fetches. It reaches back into Two thousand one hundred years of accumulated looking-at-people and it comes up holding a face, and once or twice in a very long life it has come up holding a face that already belonged to somebody. 

But those had been faces he'd seen. A man in Pompeii. A guard on Gallifrey. Strangers, in passing, filed and forgotten and then handed back with a message attached, if you were clever enough to read it. 

Rose Tyler was not a stranger. 

Rose Tyler was a living woman in a universe with a wall around it, sealed shut by his own hand, with a garden and a job and a man with one heart holding onto her — and you cannot take a face off somebody who is still using it. 

So either something had gone catastrophically wrong. 

Or something had gone extremely deliberately. 

And that was worse, because regeneration does not make mistakes. It makes choices. It always has. It simply has never, in all the centuries she'd been arguing with it, bothered to explain a single one. 

She leaned in close to the little brass mirror until her breath fogged it. 

"Why you?" she said. "Out of everything. Out of everyone. Why you, and why now?" 

The face in the mirror didn't know either. 

That was the worst of it, really. It looked just as frightened as she was. 

She smelled it before she understood it. 

That was the order of it, and the order mattered: the smell arrived first and the understanding never quite caught up. 

A scent. 

The new nose was partly to blame — everything had been arriving too loudly since the fire went out, colours shouting, the hum of the ship coming through the soles of her feet like weather — but this was not that. This was not a sharper version of something she already knew. This came from somewhere that did not have air in it. 

It was familiar. That was the trouble. It was so familiar that she caught herself turning towards it the way you turn at your own name in a crowd, and there was nothing behind her but a console and a cracked mirror lying face-down where she had put it. 

And it was full of wanting. She could find no better word, and she tried several. Something in it had been waiting a very long time. 

It felt as though it had been with her since she was a child — not pressing, not asking for anything, simply there, in the corner of every room she had ever been frightened in, the way a coat left on a chair is there. 

There was joy in it. There was grief in it, and longing, and a thin cold thread of fear running underneath, and several other things she had no names for in any of the nine languages she was fluent in and the four hundred she could get by in. 

Every smell is a key. That was the thing nobody ever told you about noses. They are not sense organs at all, not really; they are a filing system, and a brutal one, and they do not ask whether you would like the drawer opened. Love smells of something. So does hate. So does one particular afternoon you have spent two centuries declining to think about. 

So: what was this one for? 

What had she filed under this, and when, and why had she put it somewhere she could not get at? 

She never found out, because her eyes closed. 

And there was a corridor. 

Long, and narrow, and dark — and wrong, in a way it took her a moment to locate. It was longer than the space it was in. She could feel the shape of the room around it, somewhere behind the walls, and the corridor was not fitting inside it, and was not troubled about that in the slightest. 

Her head hurt. Properly hurt, the whole bowl of her skull feeling as though the seams were about to let go, and she put it down to the regeneration — they always went on aching for a day or so, that was standard, you simply got on with it — right up until she remembered the console coming at her, and the floor, and the brass frame printing a line across her palm. 

She had fallen. 

So this was a dream. 

Except that Time Lords did not dream. Not like this. They had something else, something tidier, that happened in a lower gear and could be stopped — and nothing about standing in a corridor that did not fit could be stopped, as she established by trying. 

So why was she here? 

She got out the sonic screwdriver, and thumbed it on, and held it up. 

The green light found the wall. 

There was a face on it. 

Not painted. Not projected. Simply there, the way a thing is there when you shine a light at it, looking back out of the stone with an expression she remembered wearing. 

She moved the light along. 

A second face. 

Three. Four. Five, and five was scowling at something off to the left, which was entirely in character. 

Her own. All of them her own. The lives she had already finished, set out along that wall in order like plates on a dresser, kept by somebody or something that had thought they were worth keeping. 

She walked, and the light went with her. 

Ten. 

Eleven. 

And then twelve, and twelve was only half there — the stone going vague across one side of his face, as though whoever had been doing this had been interrupted partway through, or had not been given quite enough to work with. 

She was still looking at that when somebody spoke behind her. 

She knew the voice at once. That was not the frightening part. The frightening part was the direction, because she had heard that voice for the first time about four minutes ago, coming out of her own mouth, in that flat warm London vowel she was still getting used to owning — and now it was behind her, and she was not speaking. 

"Hello." 

She turned round. 

Rose. 

Standing in a dark corridor in a dream a Time Lord should not have been having, with her hands at her sides, looking perfectly at home. 

"Why you?" the Doctor said. 

It came out before she could stop it, and it came out as a complaint, and she did not care. 

"Out of everyone. Out of every face in all those centuries of looking at people. Why did I choose you?" 

They sounded exactly alike. That was the thing she could not get past — the same pitch, the same vowels, the same slight catch before a hard consonant. Two voices that had no business existing in the same minute, saying words to each other. 

And she could not tell, looking at her, whether she was looking at Rose Tyler or at a reflection with opinions. 

"You didn't choose me," Rose said. 

She lifted her hand. There was a sonic screwdriver in it. 

She pointed it, quite gently, at the Doctor's chest. 

"I chose you." 

Both hearts went at once, hammering up into a speed she had not authorised, and the corridor let go. 

The ache drained out of her head. Her eyes opened. 

Console room. Floor. The brass mirror face-down where she had left it, and the rotor turning over, slow and easy. 

She had been asleep. 

She lay there and took that in, because it was, on the whole, more alarming than the corridor had been. Time Lords did not do this. Their minds were not built to go wandering off into the same dark that everyone else went into at night; there were doors for that and the doors were kept shut, and they stayed shut for centuries at a time. 

Unless something came and opened them. 

And then she noticed what her hearts were doing. 

Not racing. They had stopped racing. They had settled into a pattern instead, and the pattern was not one of hers. 

Four beats. Four quick ones, close together, then a space. Then four more. 

Knock. Knock. Knock. Knock. 

She put a hand flat on her chest and made them stop it. It took longer than it should have. 

Then, because she was not going to sit on the floor thinking about that, she checked the colour of her kidneys. 

And stopped. 

"Why can't I ever get the colour right?" 

She heard herself say it. She was aware, distantly, that it was an absurd thing to be worrying about four minutes into a new body on a night when something had got into her head and knocked, and she went on worrying about it anyway, because the new mind would not settle on anything for longer than a second and a half and seemed to think this was fine. 

The dream was bigger. She knew the dream was bigger. 

Minutes. She had been this person for a handful of minutes and she had already dreamed, and the dream had said something to her, and the something was still going round. 

You didn't choose me. I chose you. 

She turned it over, looking for the trick in it, the way you check a sentence for the place where the grammar has been tampered with. 

There wasn't one. It simply meant what it said, which was the worst available outcome. 

A new body. A new mind that would not hold still. A voice that belonged to a woman she had put behind a wall at the end of the universe with her own hands. And a dream that was not behaving like a dream at all, but like a message somebody had gone to considerable trouble to deliver. 

She was, she realised, extremely tired. 

Which was when the TARDIS started screaming at her. 

The alarm went off directly above her head, and underneath it something let go with a bang that she felt in her teeth. 

The floor tilted. The whole room leaned over about nine degrees and kept leaning, and every loose object in it — and there were a great many loose objects in it, because she had never once tidied up — started for the far wall at speed. 

She got a hand round the console rail on the way past. 

"Ahhh," she said. "Classic." 

The little screen swung round on its arm and told her, in the flat capitals the ship reserved for things she was not going to like: 

WARNING: TARDIS REGENERATING. NEAREST LANDING SITE SELECTED. 

Of course she was. Of course she was regenerating. The two of them had been through everything else together for a very long time, so naturally the ship had decided to do hers on the same night, at the same hour, in a manner suggesting she had been waiting politely for her turn. 

"Right," the Doctor said. "Right. Fine. Both of us. Lovely." 

She put her hands on the controls and discovered the real problem. 

She knew how to do this. She had known how to do this before she could reach the levers. The knowledge was in there — she could feel the shape of it, the whole beautiful sequence of it, sitting just out of reach like a word on the tip of a tongue that had only existed for four minutes. 

"Reduce the power," she said, out loud, hoping her hands would recognise it even if she didn't. "Keep the control rod balanced." 

Her hands went halfway and stopped. 

"...That's what I'm supposed to do. Isn't it?" 

The ship did not confirm this. 

Above her, one of the great glass spheres along the gantry went off like a bomb, and then the one next to it, and a column came down across the far side of the room in a way that columns are not meant to. 

"River would know," she said, to nobody, with some bitterness. 

She hit a red button, because it was red, and because she was out of better ideas. 

Ketchup came out from underneath the console. 

A considerable quantity of it, under real pressure, across about four feet of floor. 

The Doctor looked at it for slightly longer than the situation allowed. 

"Apparently," she said, "I have been very bored." 

She tried the next one along, a valve-shaped thing she turned on principle. 

PARTY MODE ACTIVATED. 

Confetti came down through the whole of that burning, shuddering, half-collapsed room, in a lot of colours, slowly. 

"That's not going to help," she told it. 

And then, down on the underside of the panel where she would have put something she did not want to press by accident, she found a button with a cover over it. 

"Oh, go on, then." She flipped the cover up. "Let's hope I've installed something useful." 

She pressed it. 

The outer doors opened. 

Everything in the room that had been going towards the far wall changed its mind and went for the doors instead, and so did the air, all of it, in one enormous pull — and the Doctor went with it as far as the console rail and then stopped going, because she had both arms round the console rail and had made certain decisions about letting go. 

Outside the doors there was nothing but the long bright scream of somewhere arriving too fast. 

The ship burned around her. Pieces of the gantry she had walked on four minutes ago went out past her head and into the dark. 

The door control was eighteen inches away. 

"Come on," she said, through her teeth, reaching. "Come on—" 

Her fingertips found it. 

The doors shut. 

The room fell over itself putting the air back, and she hung off the rail and breathed, and the screen chimed at her, almost apologetically. 

She dragged herself up far enough to read it. 

"Collision imminent," it said. "Ten. Nine. Eight." 

She got both arms round the column and put her head down. 

"Three. Two. One." 

Silence. 

The corridor. 

Again. 

Long, and narrow, and dark, and still not fitting inside whatever was holding it, and she was standing in it as though she had never left. 

The Doctor got the sonic out and put the light on the wall, and found that the gallery had changed. 

It was not her up there any more. 

It was a woman — average height, as far as you could tell from a face in a wall; blonde; a particular set to the mouth. Not Rose. Not anybody wearing Rose's face, either, which she checked twice, because she had been fooled once already tonight. 

Somebody else entirely. Somebody the Doctor had never seen in her life. 

She moved the light along, and there the woman was again. And again. The same face over and over, all the way down, in a corridor that went on without any apparent interest in ending. 

She walked for a long time. Long enough to stop counting. There was no sound but her own feet, and nothing on either side but that same stranger, watching her go past. 

And at last the corridor did end, and there was a flat blank wall across it. 

Something had been cut into the stone. Deep, and square, and recently, and by somebody who had wanted it read. 

LAUROLEI. 

The Doctor stood in front of it with the light on it and said it out loud, and heard her own new voice make the shape of it. 

It meant nothing to her. Nothing whatsoever. 

And that — she would think about this a good deal later, when there was time — was the single most frightening thing that had happened all night. Not the knocking. Not the woman with her own voice. A name, carved at the end of a corridor that had been built to take her to it, in a head that had been keeping files for centuries, filed under nothing at all. 

A voice spoke behind her. 

Not the woman's this time. Higher. Younger. Much younger. 

She turned round. 

There was a child standing in the corridor. 

Small, and thin, in clothes that had been worn past the point of being clothes, and holding something very carefully in one hand — a leaf, brown at the edges and gone soft the way they go when they have been carried in a pocket for a long time by somebody who did not want to lose it. 

The child looked straight at her. Not up at her. Straight at her, which was its own kind of wrong. 

"Why did you forget me?" 

The Doctor could make no sense of it. 

And yet — and this was the part her new mind would not leave alone — there was something about that child that she knew. Not the face. Something further back than the face, down where the filing had been done before she had any say in it. 

"Who are you?" she said. 

"The child you left in the middle of hell." The small voice went up and broke. "WHY DID YOU FORGET ME?" 

And at the word, the corridor came apart. 

The walls went first, the stone unknitting along the seams and falling outward into whatever was behind it, and the floor began to go as well — pieces of it simply not being there any more, spreading outward from the child's feet. 

The child kept screaming. 

"Why? WHY? WHY?" 

And every time, more of it went. 

Until there was enough gone that she could see out. 

She had assumed, because you do, that behind the walls there would be a room. There was no room. There was a sky — low and boiling and the colour of a wound, with lightning going through it sideways in long white tears, and the clouds underneath turning over and over as though something very large were moving about in them. 

"Oh, my God," the Doctor said, very quietly. "Where is this place?" 

And both hearts came close to simply stopping. 

There were not many things left in the universe that frightened her. That was not pride; it was arithmetic, and the arithmetic was grim — you cannot see what she had seen and keep a long list. But the list was not empty. 

That place was on it. 

The ground under her was not ground. It gave slightly, and it was warm, and some reflex older than this body put her hand to her mouth and then to the floor and then, idiotically, to her tongue. 

It tasted appalling. 

Her hearts were still going. 

Knock, knock, knock, knock. 

And she was getting thoroughly sick of it — this ear, this ridiculous new ear, good enough to pick up her own hearts through her own ribs and insist that she listen to them doing it. 

She opened her eyes. 

This was not the inside of the TARDIS. 

She worked that out from the sky, which was an ordinary sky, doing ordinary things, with no lightning in it at all. She was flat on her back under it, and there was grit down the side of her face. 

The ship had put her out. Somewhere in all that falling, the doors had opened and shut again around her and left her on the outside of them, which was either an accident or the single most high-handed thing that vessel had ever done to her, and she knew which way she would bet. 

She got up. Slowly, in stages, checking the joins. She brushed herself down, and turned round. 

The TARDIS was standing right behind her, scorched down one side and perfectly upright, looking — there was no other word for it — pleased with herself. 

The Doctor tried the door. 

Locked. 

"Oh, you are joking." 

She was not joking. She was regenerating, with the doors shut and the lamp dark, and she would be finished when she was finished and not a moment before. 

So. 

No ship, no answers, a name she did not know carved somewhere at the back of her own head, a child she had apparently left somewhere unforgivable, and four knocks going in her chest like somebody at a door. 

There was only one thing to be done about any of it, and she had been doing it for a very long time, and she was not going to stop on her first night in a new face. 

She looked out at the horizon, and the grin came up — the enormous one, the one that came out at the sight of a new sky. 

"Right, then," she said, in a voice she was still learning. "Let's see where the universe has brought us this time." 
